p='john_h_elliott';s='tq232_elliott_kaganparker2024'
P(p,
'A historian of early modern Spain, political power and imperial comparison. Elliott’s comparative approach sought to unsettle national exceptionalisms without dissolving political choices into impersonal structures. Kagan and Parker’s scholarly memoir describes his attraction to Febvre’s integration of a region’s history and his qualified admiration for Braudel: he valued the wider vision while doubting its integration of structural analysis with events. Political biography remained important to his Olivares project. His criticism of microhistory’s representativeness was contested in turn. Ginzburg rejected a simple opposition of microhistory to macrohistory, while Natalie Zemon Davis defended the coexistence of Martin Guerre and Martin Luther within historical inquiry. These disagreements accompany the Atlantic and composite-monarchy studies rather than making Elliott an unqualified spokesman for either large-scale synthesis or political history alone.',
'Indexed primary interview answers retain their limited scope. Newly read selected Kagan/Parker memoir pp. 163–166, 177–178, 204–206 and 231–234; original letters, reviews, memoir and critics’ original texts unread unless separately recorded. Both memoir coauthors receive credit. Braudel admiration and criticism coexist; Bloch mention alone remains insufficient for an influence edge. Death-date conflict is separately recorded: later memoir corrects earlier 10 March notice to evening 9 March 2022, citing witness evidence. Full selected books unread.',
sources=[S(s,'Richard L. Kagan and Geoffrey Parker, “John Elliott,” Biographical Memoirs of Fellows of the British Academy 21: scholarly memoir.','https://www.thebritishacademy.ac.uk/documents/4938/Memoirs-21-07-Elliot_1.pdf','Selected printed pp. 163–166, 177–178, 204–206 and 231–234 read for methods, resources, microhistory disagreement and Parker’s research formation. Note 200 explicitly corrects death date to evening 9 March 2022. Original quoted letters, private material and cited works not independently consulted; full memoir not claimed read.')],
edges=[E(p,'febvre','lucien_febvre',p,'Elliott valued Febvre’s regional synthesis as a practicable integration of social, economic and political history.',s,'Kagan/Parker pp. 165–166 and 177, quoting his comparisons. Named scholarly account, not newly read letters or Febvre book.'),E(p,'braudel',p,'fernand_braudel','Elliott questions whether Braudel’s large-scale structural history adequately connects social and economic analysis with events.',s,'Kagan/Parker p. 177 and 204; admiration for Braudel’s historical range remains. Original review and letters unread; qualified named-reader account.',kind='critique'),E(p,'ginzburg',p,'carlo_ginzburg','Questions how far Menocchio’s reconstructed case can represent a wider peasant society.',s,'Kagan/Parker pp. 204–205, their discussion of the 1980 review. Specific representativeness question; Ginzburg’s response retained.',kind='critique'),E(p,'ginzburg_reply','carlo_ginzburg',p,'Ginzburg rejects Elliott’s opposition of microhistory to macrohistory and social to political history.',s,'Kagan/Parker pp. 205–206/n143 quote the 2002 interview. Explicitly mediated; original interview unread.',kind='critique'),E(p,'davis_reply','natalie_zemon_davis',p,'Davis challenges the zero-sum opposition between histories of Martin Guerre and Martin Luther.',s,'Kagan/Parker p. 205/n144 give her 2014 reply. A specific methodological disagreement, not a denial of Elliott’s political histories.',kind='critique')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Specified selected printed pages and notes only')],holds=['Direct interview remains inaccessible; original reviews, letters and complete books unread. Parker-specific training link reserved for batch 2, when its endpoint exists.'])
