p='john_m_mackenzie';s='tq232_mackenzie_sebe2019'
P(p,
'A historian of empire’s cultural and environmental relationships. The Studies in Imperialism programme examines how empire reshaped dominant societies as well as those they ruled. Berny Sèbe’s substantive discussion situates Propaganda and Empire within that cultural intervention and describes the wider reception of MacKenzie’s environmental work. It also identifies his empirical challenge to Said’s account of Orientalism; that description is Sèbe’s reading, not independent certification of the complete 1995 book. Said’s later criticism of culture treated as propaganda remains alongside it. The Porter debate raised a further question about how evidence of imperial imagery establishes the reach of empire in British lives. That exchange’s existence and issues are established through Sèbe, while its original texts remain unread. Cultural history here is a programme of inquiry with contested categories and claims, not an automatic postcolonial or British cultural-studies affiliation.',
'Publisher series programme, earlier Frank Johnson environmental discussion and existing Said review scopes retained. Newly read Sèbe accepted manuscript selected pp. 3–5 and 14–15, especially nn. 40–46. Sèbe’s assessments are attributed; MacKenzie/Porter originals and selected books unread in full. Said’s specific propaganda criticism is not all his criticism of Hall. Original/reissue dates and the memorial’s year-only death evidence remain separate.',
sources=[S(s,'Berny Sèbe, “MacKenzie-ites without Borders,” in The MacKenzie Moment and Imperial History (2019), accepted manuscript.','https://pure-oai.bham.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/67684535/Sebe_MacKenzie_ites_withour_Borders_The_MacKenzie_Moment_and_Imperial_History_2019.pdf','Birmingham institutional accepted version; selected manuscript pp. 3–5, 14–15/nn. 40–46 read. Cultural programme, environmental reception and Said/Porter debates. Chapter’s sympathetic perspective retained; original exchanges and referenced readers unread.')],
edges=[E(p,'said',p,'edward_said','Sèbe reads MacKenzie’s empirical study of Orientalism as a challenge to generalizing Western cultural production as one imperial discourse.',s,'Accepted manuscript p. 14/n42 and 15. Named reader’s characterization, not independently read original. Existing Said-to-MacKenzie criticism coexists with this reverse disagreement.',kind='critique')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Accepted manuscript pp. 3–5 and 14–15, specified notes')],holds=['Porter has no individual endpoint; original exchange unread. Sèbe’s praise does not itself prove worldwide influence or consensus.'])

p='callum_g_brown';s='tq232_brown_atheism2017';r='tq232_brown_morris2003'
P(p,
'A social and cultural historian of religion, secularisation and unbelief. Brown’s work moved from class-based measurements of church participation toward the gendered and discursive conditions of Christian identity, with a sharp rupture proposed for the 1960s. That account remains contested. Jeremy Morris accepts criticism of inevitable modernization-to-decline stories while arguing that Brown and others narrow religion too far: institutional decline can coexist with persistent religious identity. Brown’s later “The Necessity of Atheism” approaches the problem from another direction. Using Charles Taylor as a critical foil, he argues that measuring secularity only through religious categories marginalizes lives lived without a god and that medieval disbelief should not be ruled out in advance. The intervention challenges both the assumed religious past and the categories used to describe the secular present; it does not establish a single universal measure of unbelief.',
'Primary 2017 accepted manuscript numbered pp. 1–5 and professional research statement read; rest of article and complete selected books unread. Morris’s complete publisher abstract, not full 2003 review, supplies the specified criticism. Taylor and Febvre originals not newly read; Brown’s characterizations remain attributed. Original Death 2001 retained despite profile’s 2000 shorthand; garbled citation key remains research provenance only.',
sources=[S(s,'Callum G. Brown, “The Necessity of Atheism: Making Sense of Secularisation,” Journal of Religious History 41:4 (2017): 439–456, accepted manuscript.','https://eprints.gla.ac.uk/128823/','Repository accepted manuscript numbered pp. 1–5 read, distinct from final journal pagination: definitional problem, Taylor as foil and challenge to assumed impossibility of early unbelief. No narrator testimony retained; remaining article and Taylor/Febvre originals unread.'),S(r,'Jeremy Morris, “The Strange Death of Christian Britain: Another Look at the Secularization Debate,” Historical Journal 46:4 (2003): 963–976.','https://doi.org/10.1017/S0018246X03003364','Complete author/publisher abstract read, not body. Qualified criticism of Brown, Williams and Green: institutional decline may coexist with persistent identity, and religion should not be defined too narrowly. Specific mediated abstract-level scope.')],
edges=[E(p,'taylor',p,'charles_taylor','Brown challenges Taylor’s marginalization of atheism and the religious categories through which his secular age is described.',s,'Accepted manuscript pp. 2–4. Brown’s explicit critical reading, not a finding about every Taylor argument; original A Secular Age unread.',kind='critique'),E(p,'febvre',p,'lucien_febvre','Brown contests the assumption that atheism was intellectually impossible in the early modern world.',s,'Accepted manuscript p. 5, explicitly identifying Febvre’s claim and Edwards’s counterevidence. Brown’s bounded historiographical criticism; original Febvre/Edwards texts unread.',kind='critique')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Accepted manuscript numbered pp. 1–5'),dict(source_id=r,locator='Entire displayed abstract only')],holds=['Morris lacks individual endpoint; his criticism stays in the biography. No claim of a single universally valid unbelief metric; selected books unread.'])
